---
const incidents = [
  {
    title: 'Incident 1: ACL Configuration Triggered a Software Bug and Caused a Full Network Outage',
    body: [
      'In July 2014, an ACL configuration change on the Cisco Nexus N7018 triggered a software bug, which eventually caused a full outage of the China Mobile Cloud network.',
      'After the incident, Cisco provided a technical analysis and confirmed that future ACL-related operations on the Nexus N7018 would require additional assessment to avoid triggering similar software defects.',
      'This incident highlighted the operational risk of making configuration changes on large-scale production core devices, especially when platform-specific software bugs could be triggered by routine network operations.',
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Incident 2: Syslog Configuration on Nexus 5545 Triggered a Bug and Affected Half of the Cloud Services',
    body: [
      'In November 2014, a syslog configuration change on the Cisco Nexus 5545 triggered another software bug, resulting in service disruption for approximately half of the China Mobile Cloud environment.',
      'This incident showed that even low-risk configuration changes, such as syslog adjustments, could create significant production impact when the underlying platform had software stability issues.',
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Incident 3: Full Network Outage During Nexus 5545 System Upgrade',
    body: [
      'During a system upgrade of the Nexus 5545 switches, a full network outage occurred while upgrading the second device.',
      "After the incident, Cisco's official recommendation was to suspend the upgrade process.",
      'This experience demonstrated the high operational risk involved in upgrading critical network infrastructure when the architecture lacked sufficient fault isolation or predictable failover behavior.',
    ],
  },
];

const takeaways = [
  'Troubleshooting large-scale Layer 2 and data center network issues',
  'Analyzing vPC, FEX, VRF, STP, and peer-link related risks',
  'Handling high-impact production incidents under pressure',
  'Working with vendor support teams such as Cisco TAC',
  'Evaluating the operational risks of network architecture design',
  'Creating post-incident optimization and risk mitigation plans',
];
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Case study on operating and troubleshooting a large-scale Cisco Nexus-based cloud network using Nexus 7018, 5545, 2048, vPC, FEX, VRF, and STP."
    />
    <meta name="generator" content={Astro.generator} />
    <title>Cisco Nexus Cloud Network Operations | Dongxu Allen Hu</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="site-header">
      <a class="brand" href="/">Dongxu (Allen) Hu</a>
      <nav aria-label="Main navigation">
        <a href="/#experience">Experience</a>
        <a href="/#projects">Case Studies</a>
        <a href="/photography/">Photography</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <main>
      <section class="case-hero">
        <p class="eyebrow">Case Study</p>
        <h1>Operating and Troubleshooting a Large-Scale Cisco Nexus-Based Cloud Network</h1>
        <p>
          During my time working on the China Mobile Cloud project at the Southern Base, I was responsible for operating
          and maintaining a large-scale cloud network architecture built on Cisco Nexus N7018, Nexus 5545, and Nexus
          2048 devices.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="case-grid">
        <article class="case-card primary">
          <p class="eyebrow">Architecture Context</p>
          <p>
            The architecture used several key Cisco Nexus technologies, including vPC, FEX, and VRF. At the time I left
            the project, the number of VRF instances had reached the platform limit of 4,096. However, the architecture
            did not use VDC, one of the major features of the Cisco Nexus 7000 series.
          </p>
          <p>
            Based on my operational experience, the architecture presented several challenges in terms of stability,
            maintainability, and change risk. Below are several major incidents I personally experienced during
            production operations.
          </p>
        </article>

        <aside class="case-card impact">
          <p class="eyebrow">Platform Scope</p>
          <strong>Cisco Nexus cloud network</strong>
          <span>Nexus N7018, Nexus 5545, Nexus 2048</span>
          <span>vPC, FEX, VRF, STP, peer-link behavior</span>
          <span>4,096 VRF instances at platform limit</span>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section class="incident-list">
        {incidents.map((incident) => (
          <article class="case-card">
            <p class="eyebrow">Production Incident</p>
            <h2>{incident.title}</h2>
            {incident.body.map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}
          </article>
        ))}
      </section>

      <section class="case-grid">
        <article class="case-card">
          <p class="eyebrow">Technical Analysis</p>
          <p>
            Based on the symptoms observed during these incidents, the main architectural risks appeared to be related
            to the interaction between vPC peer-link behavior, STP, and the broader Layer 2 design.
          </p>
          <p>
            In theory, once the Cisco Nexus-based virtualized network architecture is running properly, STP should play a
            reduced role because the purpose of the architecture is to eliminate Layer 2 loops. However, in practice,
            disabling or changing STP behavior could also create issues when vPC comes online.
          </p>
          <p>
            As a result, the architecture became extremely sensitive to configuration changes, software upgrades, and
            device reloads. Even routine operations required careful risk assessment.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="case-card">
          <p class="eyebrow">Key Takeaways</p>
          <p>
            This experience gave me deep hands-on exposure to operating a large-scale Cisco Nexus-based cloud network in
            a production environment.
          </p>
          <ul>
            {takeaways.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
          </ul>
          <p>
            This case strengthened my understanding that in large-scale data center networks, stability depends not only
            on advanced technologies, but also on architecture simplicity, clear failure domains, software maturity, and
            strict change management.
          </p>
        </article>
      </section>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>

